<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shopping at the Market – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em; color: #333;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Is the Pineapple?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Explain the main ideas of class readings to absent students. concept: Use examples to explain math concepts to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the main ideas from a class reading about the market with basic words and gestures (e.g., points to apples and says, 'We buy apples at the market.' 'They are cheap.'). Can use objects to explain quantities and prices (e.g., holds two apples and says, 'These are two apples.' or 'The apples cost two dollars.'). Can use simple counting with classmates to show math concepts (e.g., holds up three fingers and says, 'three apples').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the Mediation skill in the context of the Market Shopping project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of market items (pineapple, apples, potatoes, cassava, money). The teacher points to each item and asks, "What is this?" and "How much is it?" Students respond with simple answers like "It's an apple," or "It's two dollars." The teacher quickly reviews numbers 1-100 with simple counting. (5 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining a simple market transaction to an imaginary 'absent friend.' The teacher holds up a flashcard of a pineapple and two dollar bills. "My friend was absent. I need to explain. This is a pineapple. It costs two dollars. We buy pineapples at the market." The teacher uses clear pronunciation for /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money,' and blends sounds for 'two.' The teacher uses gestures to show 'two.' (3 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the mediation concept.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a friend was not here yesterday, how can I help them understand about the market?" (Expected: "Tell them," "Show them.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I use my hands to show 'three apples'?" (Expected: "Yes!")</li>
                    </ul>
                (2 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated story (narrative excerpt, from Lesson 2) or a short dialogue about "Shopping at the Market" with prices for various items. The register is informal and simple. (e.g., "Yesterday, Maria went to the market. She wanted three apples. The apples were cheap. They cost one dollar each. She also needed five potatoes. How much were the potatoes?") The text will explicitly use vocabulary like 'market,' 'pineapple,' 'potatoes,' 'buy,' 'cost,' 'cheap,' 'expensive,' and numbers up to 100. (5 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand simple descriptions of shopping experiences and prices. They recognize key market vocabulary and numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Where did Maria go?" (Expected: "To the market.") "What did she buy?" (Expected: "Apples and potatoes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The apples were expensive." (False). "She needed five potatoes." (True).</li>
                    </ul>
                (5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation skills to explain market concepts and prices.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining main ideas and math concepts (quantities, prices) using WH-Questions ('How much is it?'), Quantities ('I need five potatoes.'), Present Simple ('I want three apples.'), and polite expressions like 'Excuse me.' Focus on pronunciation of /p/, /m/, and number blending.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice: "Market Price Explanations."</b> The teacher displays flashcards of market items with their prices (e.g., 'pineapple - $2', 'apples - $1 each', 'potatoes - $5 for 5'). Students complete sentences orally or on mini-whiteboards: "The ____ costs ____ dollars." The teacher then asks students to explain these to a partner as if they were an absent friend: "The pineapple costs two dollars." using gestures. (5 minutes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled: "Explain the Quantity."</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has small plastic fruits/vegetables or drawings. Student A picks a number of items (e.g., three apples) and says, "I need three apples." Student B, acting as the 'mediator,' explains to an imaginary 'peer' what Student A needs, holding up three fingers and saying, "She needs three apples." They practice different quantities and items from the vocabulary list. (5 minutes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction: "Shopping List Explanation."</b> Students receive a simple shopping list (e.g., "Shopping List: 2 bananas, 1 pineapple, 4 potatoes"). In pairs, Student A "reads" the list to Student B. Student B (the mediator) must explain what's on the list to an 'absent classmate' using simple sentences, gestures, and drawing if needed. For example, "First, we need two bananas!" (shows two fingers). "Then, one pineapple." "And four potatoes!" Students switch roles. (5 minutes)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE mediation skills in a realistic market scenario, connecting to the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Explaining the Market Role-Play."</b> This activity serves as the culmination of Project 1: Market Shopping Role-Play. Students will work in small groups of three. One student will be the 'shopper,' another the 'seller,' and the third will be the 'mediator' who explains the interaction to an imaginary 'new student' or 'younger sibling' who has never been to a market. The shopper and seller perform a basic transaction (e.g., "Excuse me, how much is the pineapple?" "It's three dollars." "I want one pineapple."). The mediator's job is to explain *what is happening* using simple language, gestures, and pointing to objects. For example, "The shopper wants a pineapple. She asks 'How much is it?' The seller says 'Three dollars.' She will buy one." Mediators must use target vocabulary (market, price, buy, sell, item, money, numbers), WH-questions, quantities, and polite expressions. (10 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of mediation skills and connection to the project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher will circulate and use a simple checklist to observe each 'mediator' in the group activities from Stage 4. Criteria will include: intelligible pronunciation, use of target vocabulary, clarity of explanation, use of gestures/objects, and ability to convey the main idea of the market transaction or concept.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Mediation Demonstration."</b> Each small group from Stage 4 will perform a brief 1-minute demonstration. The 'mediator' explains to the class (acting as new students) one key aspect of the "Market Shopping Role-Play" project, such as "How to ask for the price," or "How to tell someone what you want to buy." They use realia or flashcards. (5 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /p/, /m/, and numbers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'market,' 'price,' 'how much,' 'buy,' 'sell,' specific item names, numbers 1-100).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation (can the listener understand the main idea?).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures and objects to aid understanding.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration with group members (if applicable).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences orally or in writing:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I helped my friends understand _______."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain 'how much is it' using _______."</li>
                    </ul>
                (3 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to explain the market game to a friend?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words did I use to help my friend understand?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What mediation strategies worked best for 4th graders (gestures, simple sentences, objects)? Were students able to explain main ideas from the market reading and math concepts effectively? What specific language points (WH-questions, quantities) need more reinforcement for clarity in explanation? What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to mediate?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Shopping at the Market" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly reviewing market vocabulary or applying number skills in a different context, building on the communicative and mediation skills developed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>